{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be your doctor during your stay. Can you tell me what brought you in today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, thanks. I’ve had a sore throat for about three days, and it’s been really hard to swallow—especially liquids. I keep coughing after I eat or drink."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:52:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Any fever, shortness of breath, or chest pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:53:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, no fever or trouble breathing. Just this choking feeling when I swallow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. And your trach—any secretions or leakage around it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:55:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, it’s been dry. I’ve had trouble with swallowing before, but it seemed to be getting better until about two weeks ago when it got worse again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:56:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Got it. We’ve reviewed your history—tracheobronchomalacia, laryngeal stenosis, and your tracheostomy since infancy. We’ll need to figure out why you’re having this issue now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:57:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had a swallow study a while back and they didn’t find anything. I’m worried it’s getting worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:58:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s helpful to know. We’ve already done a chest X-ray, and it looks clear—no pneumonia or blockage. Your labs are normal too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2169-06-12 17:59:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not an infection?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2169-06-12 18:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Doesn’t look like it. The interventional pulmonology team saw you in the ER and didn’t think you needed a bronchoscopy. But we’ve scheduled a speech and swallow evaluation tomorrow morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2169-06-12 18:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Should I keep avoiding liquids?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2169-06-12 18:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "For now, stick to soft foods and thickened liquids. The speech team will give you specific guidance. We also want to do an EGD—just a camera down your esophagus—to make sure there’s no blockage or irritation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2169-06-12 18:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that hurt?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2169-06-12 18:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll be sedated, so you won’t feel it. It’s just to rule out anything we can’t see from the outside."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. The speech and swallow team evaluated you earlier. How did it go?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "They had me try different textures. I did better when I covered my trach while swallowing. They said that helps me use my throat muscles better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. That’s a great strategy. They said you handled regular food well with that technique. No signs of aspiration."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. So why do I feel like I’m choking?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They think it’s due to pharyngeal residue—some food or liquid lingering in your throat after swallowing. Covering your trach helps generate more pressure, clearing it better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Ah, that actually makes sense. I do feel like something’s stuck."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also did the EGD this morning while you were sedated. I just reviewed the report—your esophagus and stomach look completely normal. No strictures, ulcers, or blockages."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So nothing’s physically wrong?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not structurally, no. Your symptoms—this globus sensation, the feeling of choking—are likely due to how your throat is functioning, especially with your history of airway issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is this related to my GERD?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Possibly. Even though you’re on omeprazole and ranitidine, silent reflux can irritate the throat. But the speech team’s strategy should help a lot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I should keep covering my trach when I eat?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2169-06-13 09:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s the key recommendation. They cleared you for a regular diet as long as you do that. We’ll keep monitoring, but you’re doing well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your vitals are stable, lungs are clear. Just a few mild wheezes, but that’s usual for you. How’s your swallowing today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. I had soup this morning and covered my trach like they said. No coughing!"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s excellent. We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow morning if this continues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. Should I keep doing anything special at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep covering your trach when eating, stay on your current meds—especially the reflux ones—and continue your nebulizer treatments for your lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my gabapentin for pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue that as prescribed. And fluoxetine for your mood—important to keep that steady."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need another swallow study?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2169-06-14 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. But your primary doctor will follow up to make sure you’re maintaining your weight and tolerating food. If symptoms return, we may reconsider."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2169-06-15 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you’re cleared to go home today. Your swallowing has improved, and all tests were normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2169-06-15 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel so much better knowing it’s not something serious."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2169-06-15 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll send instructions to your primary doctor. Keep using the trach-covering technique, take your meds, and come back if you can’t keep food down or start losing weight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2169-06-15 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for explaining everything so clearly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2169-06-15 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and we’re here if you need us."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2169-06-12 17:45:00",
    "admission_end": "2169-06-15 16:35:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A young woman with a history of tracheobronchomalacia, laryngeal stenosis, bronchopulmonary dysplasia, and tracheostomy dependence was admitted for evaluation of worsening dysphagia and odynophagia. She reported a three-day history of sore throat and difficulty swallowing, with coughing after liquids. Initial workup showed normal labs and a clear chest X-ray. Speech and swallow evaluation revealed improved swallowing with tracheostomy occlusion, and an EGD showed no mechanical obstruction. Her symptoms were attributed to pharyngeal residue, and she was successfully managed with behavioral strategies. She tolerated a regular diet with technique modification, remained stable, and was discharged home with follow-up instructions.",
    "problems": [
      "Dysphagia, unspecified",
      "Acute pharyngitis, unspecified",
      "Tracheobronchomalacia",
      "Laryngeal Stenosis",
      "Bronchopulmonary dysplasia originating in the perinatal period",
      "Unspecified asthma, uncomplicated",
      "Unspecified chronic bronchitis",
      "Gastro-esophageal reflux disease without esophagitis",
      "Other chronic pain",
      "Major depressive disorder, single episode, unspecified",
      "Anxiety disorder, unspecified",
      "Tracheostomy status"
    ]
  }
}
